<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hans Egede</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1686">1686</a>–<a href="/years/1758">1758</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hans Egede was a Norwegian Lutheran missionary who sailed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greenland">Greenland</a> in 1721 hoping to find the descendants of the medieval Norse settlers. Finding only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inuit">Inuit</a>, he founded a mission and trading post at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuuk">Godthåb</a> (Nuuk) in 1728 and learned their language. His work began Danish-Norwegian colonisation of Greenland, and he is known as the Apostle of Greenland.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hans-egede.jpg" alt="Hans Egede" width="320" height="417" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Johan Hörner · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hans_Poulsen_Egede.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hans Poulsen Egede was born on 31 January 1686 at Harstad in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, far north of the Arctic Circle. His mother was Kirsten Jensdatter Hind, a local merchant's daughter, and his father Povel Hansen Egede was a civil servant. An uncle, a clergyman, schooled him, and in 1704 he went to the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a>, where he earned a bachelor's degree in theology. He was ordained on 15 April 1707 and given a parish in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lofoten">Lofoten</a>. There he heard stories of the old Norse settlements in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greenland">Greenland</a>, with which contact had been lost centuries before. From 1711 he asked Frederick IV of Denmark-Norway for permission to search for the colony and found a mission.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Egede founded the Bergen Greenland Company with capital from Bergen merchants. The ship Haabet and two smaller ones left Bergen on 2 May 1721 with Egede, his wife Gertrud, four children and forty other colonists, and on 3 July they founded Hope Colony at Nuup Kangerlua. Searching for months for Norse descendants, he found only the Kalaallit and began learning their language. Many colonists suffered scurvy and most went home. His 1723 expedition found the churches and ruins of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-settlement">Eastern Settlement</a>, though he took them for the Western one. </p><p>In 1724 he baptised his first child converts. In 1728 a royal expedition under Major Claus Paarss moved the colony to the mainland and built the fort Godt-Haab, the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuuk">Godthåb</a>. His book The Old Greenland's New Perlustration appeared in 1729.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> epidemic raged among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inuit">Inuit</a> from 1734, and in 1735 it took Gertrud. Egede carried her body back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, leaving his son Poul to continue the work. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a> he became superintendent of the Greenland Mission Seminary and in 1741 Lutheran Bishop of Greenland. He died on 5 November 1758 at Stubbekøbing on Falster. He is remembered as the Apostle of Greenland, credited with reviving Danish-Norwegian interest in the island, and the Royal Danish Geographical Society founded the Egede Medal in his honour in 1916. His son Poul published the four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">Gospels</a> in Greenlandic in 1744.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hans Egede” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The missionary <b>Hans Egede</b> began the Danish recolonisation of Greenland in 1721, the origin of the present link between Denmark and Greenland.</blockquote><a href="/empire/denmark-norway">Denmark-Norway · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Hoping to find the lost Norse colonists, the Norwegian priest <b>Hans Egede</b> sailed to Greenland in 1721 with the support of Denmark-Norway and founded a mission and trading post, moving it to Godthåb (Nuuk) in 1728.</blockquote><a href="/state/danish-greenland">Danish Greenland</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Harstad Municipality, in what is now <a href="/countries/norway">Norway</a>. Died in Stubbekøbing, in what is now <a href="/countries/denmark">Denmark</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=68.8,16.54&amp;year=1732" aria-label="Harstad Municipality on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4866 0 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Hans Egede"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5316 226h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5316 226h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=68.8,16.54&amp;year=1732">What was here in 1732? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
